1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:07,400
CrisisLens: disaster triage with a small AI model, trained with Adaption Labs AutoScientist.

2
00:00:08,050 --> 00:00:13,495
When a disaster strikes, response teams are flooded with thousands of social-media posts.

3
00:00:13,495 --> 00:00:17,519
Hidden among them are people asking for rescue, water, or medicine.

4
00:00:17,519 --> 00:00:20,402
Reading every post by hand is far too slow.

5
00:00:21,052 --> 00:00:27,336
CrisisLens reads each post and returns a strict JSON triage record: the humanitarian category,

6
00:00:27,336 --> 00:00:32,929
whether it is actionable, an urgency score from one to five, the location, the needs,

7
00:00:32,929 --> 00:00:34,676
and a short rationale.

8
00:00:35,326 --> 00:00:37,793
This is the CrisisLens triage console.

9
00:00:37,793 --> 00:00:43,262
It replays real posts from four disasters that the model never saw during training.

10
00:00:43,912 --> 00:00:49,190
As posts arrive, each triage record joins a priority queue ranked by urgency,

11
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:52,082
so the most critical posts rise to the top.

12
00:00:52,082 --> 00:00:58,232
The dashboard tracks volume, actionable posts, life-safety posts, and the category mix.

13
00:00:58,882 --> 00:01:04,682
Responders can filter the queue to critical posts only, with urgency four or higher.

14
00:01:05,332 --> 00:01:10,590
Selecting a post opens its full record, with the location, needs, rationale,

15
00:01:10,590 --> 00:01:13,604
and the model's latency on a laptop CPU.

16
00:01:14,254 --> 00:01:16,819
The Analyze tab triages a single post.

17
00:01:16,819 --> 00:01:22,593
Here, families trapped by flood water are flagged as an urgent, life-threatening request,

18
00:01:22,593 --> 00:01:25,358
with the location and needs extracted.

19
00:01:26,008 --> 00:01:31,248
A hospital running out of insulin and power is marked actionable, with medical, water,

20
00:01:31,248 --> 00:01:32,504
and power needs.

21
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:39,818
And a message of sympathy is correctly given the lowest urgency, so it does not distract responders.

22
00:01:40,468 --> 00:01:46,205
The model was trained on HumAID: 76,484 human-labelled tweets from 19 disasters.

23
00:01:46,205 --> 00:01:49,940
We removed duplicates and split the data by disaster.

24
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:56,704
Four disasters were held out completely: the Kerala floods, Cyclone Idai, the Mexico earthquake,

25
00:01:56,704 --> 00:01:58,484
and Hurricane Dorian.

26
00:01:59,134 --> 00:02:03,978
We fine-tuned Qwen 3.5 2B with LoRA, using Adaption Labs AutoScientist.

27
00:02:03,978 --> 00:02:08,789
The default recipe failed, so we fixed the prompt and the learning rate,

28
00:02:08,789 --> 00:02:12,230
and AutoScientist then tuned the recipe itself.

29
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:17,588
In total we ran eight AutoScientist training runs, testing 2, 4,

30
00:02:17,588 --> 00:02:22,712
and 9 billion parameter models and up to 49 thousand training rows.

31
00:02:23,362 --> 00:02:25,148
Run five is our best model.

32
00:02:25,148 --> 00:02:30,457
It shares the top validation score, and it is the model we evaluated on the test set.

33
00:02:30,457 --> 00:02:34,442
The test set was used only once, so the results are honest.

34
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:43,630
On 1,219 posts from the unseen disasters, CrisisLens reaches a macro-F1 of 0.715.

35
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:51,130
The best frontier API model reaches 0.606, a classic TF-IDF model 0.662,

36
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:55,388
and the untuned base model only 0.427.

37
00:02:56,038 --> 00:03:01,207
For urgent requests for help, CrisisLens finds 76 percent of them.

38
00:03:01,207 --> 00:03:04,502
The frontier API finds only 32 percent.

39
00:03:05,152 --> 00:03:10,045
It is also practical: 99.9 percent of its outputs are valid JSON,

40
00:03:10,045 --> 00:03:15,728
and it runs on a laptop CPU in about five seconds per post, at zero cost.

41
00:03:16,378 --> 00:03:18,316
We are open about its limits.

42
00:03:18,316 --> 00:03:22,816
Its most common mistake is treating donation offers as urgent requests.

43
00:03:22,816 --> 00:03:27,950
The hosted demo replays recorded outputs, because live hosting needs a paid plan.

44
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:29,834
Live triage runs locally.

45
00:03:30,484 --> 00:03:36,042
CrisisLens: a small, free model that beats frontier APIs at disaster triage.

46
00:03:36,042 --> 00:03:39,418
The demo, the model, and all the code are open.

47
00:03:39,418 --> 00:03:40,436
Thank you.

